clean_summary:
I remain pretty unconvinced that there are any quantum chemistry problems that are both relevant to the real world and only capable of being tackled by quantum computers.

Garnet Chan and his team used classical computers to achieve a task many researchers thought would be impossible without a quantum computer: They computed a key property of nitrogenase, one of the most important life-supporting molecules on Earth. Chan hopes that the result might encourage others to tackle related problems without waiting for a quantum computer.

@QuantaMagazine: "Do we need quantum computers to fully understand complex chemical reactions? A new result, decades in the making, shows the surprising power of ordinary 'classical' machines."

